<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/foundational/how-language-works">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    There are just over 7,000 languages alive today, and every one of them does the same
    impossible job: packing infinite thoughts into a finite toolbox of sounds and rules. You
    can build a sentence nobody in history has ever uttered — "the octopus filed a complaint
    about the lift music" — and any adult speaker of English will understand it instantly,
    without effort, without instruction. That ability is the central mystery of linguistics,
    and the answer this lesson builds toward is surprising: what you are doing when you speak
    is not consulting a list of memorised sentences, but running a <em>system</em> — a complex
    but law-governed mental system, as the introductory lecture puts it. Language lives in the
    mind as rules and patterns, not as a phrasebook.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three facts show how deep the system runs. First, it is <strong>implicit</strong>: a
    five-year-old commands English word order flawlessly but cannot state the rule; you
    "just know" that the big red ball is fine and the red big ball is wrong, without ever
    being taught. Second, it is <strong>law-governed</strong>: the patterns are statistical
    and systematic, not loose habits — every language in the world orders its sentence
    elements in ways that follow discoverable universals. Third, it is <strong>creative</strong>:
    from a finite toolbox, an infinite range — the property Wilhelm von Humboldt called
    making "infinite use of finite means".
  </p>
  <p>
    To see how the trick works, we build language up layer by layer — sound, meaning, and the
    rulebook that connects them — then look at what all languages share, how writing evolved
    to capture speech, and how languages grow, change and die.
  </p>

  <h2>Phonemes: meaningless sound, meaningful difference</h2>
  <p>
    The bottom layer is sound, and the crucial unit is not the letter but the
    <strong>phoneme</strong> — the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning. Say "pat",
    "bat", "cat": the difference between p, b and c is all that separates three words, so each
    is a distinct phoneme of English. English runs on about 44 phonemes squeezed into 26
    letters — hence the spelling chaos, where "shun" can be written -tion, -sion, -cian or
    just -shun. Phoneme inventories vary enormously across languages: English has about 24
    consonants and 20 vowels; Hawaiian manages with 13 sounds in total; some Khoisan languages
    deploy dozens of click consonants. Two facts matter for the bigger picture. One: phonemes
    are themselves <em>meaningless</em> — the sound "k" means nothing alone. Two: which sound
    differences count is language-specific — the breathy difference between English "p" in
    "pin" and "spin" is meaningful in Hindi but invisible to most English speakers, who
    literally struggle to hear it as an adult.
  </p>

  <h2>Morphemes and words: the smallest pieces of meaning</h2>
  <p>
    Stack phonemes and you reach the first meaningful units: <strong>morphemes</strong>.
    "Un-believ-abl-y" contains four: <em>un-</em> (not), <em>believe</em> (the root),
    <em>-able</em> (capable of being), <em>-ly</em> (in the manner of). Languages differ wildly
    in how much work morphemes do. English is fairly "analytic" — it strings separate words
    together ("will not have been going"). Latin, Turkish or Swahili are "synthetic" and pack
    entire clauses into one word: Turkish <em>evlerinizden</em> means "from your houses" in a
    single morpheme-packed unit. The vocabulary of an average educated adult runs to tens of
    thousands of words, yet every one of them was acquired from context — you learned most of
    your language without ever looking it up, which is itself evidence of how rule-like the
    acquisition machinery is.
  </p>

  <h2>Syntax and semantics: the rulebook and the meanings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Syntax</strong> is the layer people mean by "grammar": the rules for assembling
    morphemes and words into phrases and sentences. Its defining property is
    <strong>recursion</strong> — rules that can apply to their own output. Take "the dog
    barked", embed it as a relative clause, embed that in another: "the cat that chased the
    rat that stole the cheese that sat in the house that Jack built". Nothing in principle
    stops the loop, which is how a finite rulebook generates infinite sentences. Word order is
    syntax at its most visible: English is subject–verb–object (the dog bit the man), Japanese
    is subject–object–verb, Welsh verb–subject–object — yet all three build questions,
    negations and relative clauses with the same underlying logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Semantics</strong> is the layer that attaches meaning, and it is compositional:
    the meaning of a sentence is built from the meanings of its parts plus the way syntax
    combines them. "The dog bit the man" and "The man bit the dog" contain identical words and
    opposite events — the syntax <em>is</em> part of the meaning. Ambiguity shows the layers
    working independently: "I saw the man with the telescope" has two readings because one
    string of words maps onto two different structures. Pragmatics goes a level higher, to
    meaning in context — "Can you pass the salt?" is not a question about ability.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "grammar" as taught in school — never split an infinitive,
    never end a sentence with a preposition — is largely 18th-century taste imported from
    Latin, not the actual rule system. The scientific position is <strong>descriptivist</strong>:
    linguists study how people <em>do</em> speak, and the real grammar is the implicit system
    every native speaker already commands. "Ain't nobody got time for that" is a perfectly
    formed sentence of a real dialect — with a negation system English has used for centuries.
  </p>

  <h2>What all languages share</h2>
  <p>
    Strip away vocabulary and every language, spoken or signed, shows the same design
    features (the linguist Charles Hockett's classic list, distilled):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Productivity.</strong> You have never heard most sentences you can instantly understand or build. No animal communication system does this — bee dances and vervet alarm calls are fixed inventories, not generative systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Displacement.</strong> We talk about yesterday, tomorrow, and things that don't exist. Language escapes the here-and-now.</li>
    <li><strong>Duality of patterning.</strong> A small inventory of meaningless sounds combines into vast meaningful vocabularies — the trick that makes writing, and then typing, possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Arbitrariness.</strong> The link between sound and meaning is conventional — "dog", "chien", "perro", "inu". (Onomatopoeia is the tiny exception that proves the rule.)</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural transmission and learnability.</strong> No child is born speaking Khmer or Quechua; any infant raised in any language community acquires that language on roughly the same schedule — including deaf children acquiring sign languages, which are full languages with their own phonology and syntax, not gestures.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Writing: five millennia of freezing speech</h2>
  <p>
    Speech is maybe 100,000+ years old; writing is about five. When it was finally invented —
    apparently independently in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica — it began not with
    poetry but with accounting: Sumerian clay tablets recording grain and sheep, because
    remembered debts are deniable debts.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five millennia of writing — from Sumerian receipts to a script for every language" widget="timeline" config={writeTimeline} />

  <p>
    The timeline shows the real evolutionary logic: each step is a compression of the
    sound–sign mapping. Pictograms draw things; word-signs stand for words; the rebus trick
    borrows signs for their sound alone ("I ♥ you"); the alphabet takes the final step of one
    sign per phoneme — a couple of dozen symbols instead of hundreds. That compression is why
    alphabetic literacy can be learned by almost anyone in months rather than years, and why
    the Greek addition of vowels (borrowing consonant signs Phoenician didn't need) made
    writing a complete transcription of speech. The 1991 Unicode milestone completes the
    story: one standard encoding every script on Earth, so the writing system is finally
    independent of any single language or machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writing systems themselves sort into a spectrum, and most real scripts are hybrids.
    <strong>Logographic</strong> systems (Chinese characters) map signs to words or
    morphemes — thousands to learn, but compact and readable across dialects and even
    across mutually unintelligible spoken languages. <strong>Syllabic</strong> systems
    (Japanese kana, Linear B) map signs to syllables — dozens to hundreds of symbols.
    <strong>Alphabetic</strong> systems map signs to phonemes — the cheapest to learn,
    as the timeline showed. Japanese illustrates the hybrid strategy: kanji carry the
    content words while kana carry the grammar, a division of labour between system
    types inside one sentence. The choice of system is history, not quality: Korean
    moved from Chinese characters to its featural hangul alphabet (1443, consciously
    designed), while Chinese stayed logographic for cultural continuity — both work;
    they price literacy differently.
  </p>

  <h2>Families, change and death</h2>
  <p>
    Languages are never still. Pronunciation drifts, meanings shift ("nice" once meant
    foolish), and populations split — and over centuries the drift compounds into dialects,
    then separate languages. English, Hindi, Spanish, Bengali, Russian and Greek are all
    cousins in the <strong>Indo-European family</strong>, descended from a common ancestor
    spoken roughly 5,500 years ago on the Pontic steppe; about 46% of humanity speaks its
    descendant languages today. How do we know? Because the correspondences are
    <em>systematic</em>, not accidental. Grimm's law (1822) showed Latin <em>p</em>
    consistently corresponds to English <em>f</em> (pater/father, piscis/fish, ped/foot) —
    a regular sound change, not a coincidence, and the kind of rule that lets linguists
    reconstruct dead ancestor languages like Proto-Indo-European from living descendants.
  </p>
  <p>
    The family tree is now pruning itself. Roughly half of today's 7,000 languages have fewer
    than about 7,000 speakers and are endangered; a language dies roughly every few weeks.
    Every loss takes with it an untranslatable archive — ecological knowledge, oral history,
    a way of carving up the world. Revitalisation efforts (Welsh, Māori, Hawaiian immersion
    schools) show decline is not inevitable; it follows prestige and policy, and both can be
    changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest question in the field is <em>how the system gets into the child's head</em>.
    Children say things they have never heard ("goed", "two foots") — evidence they are
    applying rules, not copying input. The poverty-of-the-stimulus argument holds that the
    data a child hears is too sparse to explain the grammar they converge on, so the mind must
    bring strong built-in expectations (Chomsky's Universal Grammar). Critics respond that
    statistical learning from rich input, plus general cognition, can do the job; the debate —
    nature versus nurture for grammar — remains genuinely open, with modern usage-based
    accounts and generative accounts still fighting it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other expert edges: <strong>pidgins and creoles</strong> — when adult speakers with no
    common language improvise a bare contact pidgin, their children convert it into a full
    creole with regular grammar within one generation, the closest thing nature offers to a
    grammar-creation experiment. <strong>Sign languages</strong> prove the sound layer is not
    essential: they have phonemes (handshape, location, movement) and duality of patterning
    with no sound at all. <strong>Writing ≠ language</strong>: speech is primary biologically;
    literacy is a cultural technology bolted on, which is why dyslexia can impair reading
    while leaving spoken language intact. And the origin question — how recursion and
    displacement first evolved — remains one of the great open problems, constrained but
    unanswered by primate communication, fossil anatomy and genetics (FOXP2 among other genes).
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ethnologue (current language census); Hockett, "The Origin of Speech" (1960); Crystal, Language Death; Chomsky, Aspects of the Theory of Syntax; Renfrew &amp; Bellwood on Indo-European dispersal.</p>
